        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Edge devices represent a pool of both computational power and data for ML tasks. In order to access this pool in a manner which minimizes communication costs while preserving privacy, McMahan et al. (2017) introduced Federated Learning (FL). Their FL algorithm, FedAvg, achieved these aims by training ML models directly on client devices and then performing aggregation over model weights, thus bypassing the need to share training data. As the data distribution may differ considerably across clients, emphasizing global model convergence leads to a trade-off between federated performance and performance on client data partitions. In some cases, a federated model may have lower accuracy on a client than a local model trained from scratch. This eliminates one of the main incentives for the client to participate in the FL process.\nThe existing body of work on balancing the trade-off between federated and local performance has focused on two primary means of improving accuracy on heterogeneous data distributions. Li et al. (2019a) propose Q-Fair Federated Learning (Q-FFL) as a branch of FL which tunes the aggregate objective function to focus on clients with large losses when creating a single shared global model. This is done in an attempt to smooth-out the accuracy distribution across client data partitions. Alternatively, local adaptation (personalization) methods such as Freezebase (FB), Multi-task Learning (MTL) and Knowledge Distillation (KD) have been recommended by Yu et al. (2020) and Mansour et al. (2020) as a means of quickly constructing effective local models by using the federated model as a starting point. Since local adaptation requires further on-device training, the natural baseline of comparison is a fully local model trained on the specific client for a comparable amount of time. On the other hand, Li et al. (2019a) evaluates Q-FFL in terms of the absolute accuracy distribution of the federated model over local data partitions, without regard to the potential performance of a fully\nlocal model for a client. For the rest of this work, relative accuracy shall refer to the difference in achieved accuracy between a federated and local model on a client test set.\nWhile the sets of potential use cases for these techniques are not identical\u2014for instance, personalization would be inappropriate when clients do not have sufficient data for re-training\u2014, it is possible that using Q-FFL may result in a fairer relative accuracy distribution without significantly hurting average performance. Locally adapting on a client requires sufficiently powerful hardware to retrain it, high-quality data, and a large amount of dedicated training time. If Q-FFL can reduce the need for personalization by addressing the same concerns, it may be considered preferable for a federated network whose clients do not fulfil the aforementioned requirements. For this to be the case, Q-FFL would need to lower the number of underperforming clients or improve their average relative accuracy enough so that they require less adaptation. This is not what we observe in practice, as our experiments show Q-FFL to have either a negative impact on relative accuracy or none at all.\nOur contribution is threefold. First, we construct an initial empirical evaluation of the relative accuracy distribution of models trained with Q-FFL on the Reddit, CIFAR-10, and FEMNIST datasets for either next word prediction or image recognition. We then compare the effects of Q-FFL on the number of underperforming clients to a FedAvg baseline. During our evaluation, we show that Q-FFL does not significantly improve the relative accuracy distribution for next word prediction and brings little discernible benefit over a combination of FedAvg and local adaptation. Concerningly, it doubled the number of underperforming clients for image recognition on FEMNIST.\nSecond, we investigate any potential synergies between Q-FFL and personalization. We adapt fair federated models and show that the adapted models do not outperform those initially trained with FedAvg on the language task in terms of either average accuracy or the number of underperforming clients. Effects for the image recognition task are unclear for both datasets as the potential effects are too small to evaluate clearly.\nThird, we propose using Knowledge Distillation (Hinton et al., 2015) during local training after the halfway point of the FL process as an alternative to Q-FFL. Preliminary experimental results on the language task show a significant reduction in the number of underperforming clients when compared to Q-FFL without any downsides to subsequent local adaptation. This technique also avoids the increase in the number of underperforming clients observed for image recognition on FEMNIST. We speculate that Knowledge Distillation outperforms the loss-based weighted averaging mechanism of Q-FFL because it is able to better compromise between the data partitions of different clients, although further research is required to test this hypothesis."
